On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:55:15 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I think that's ugly, and also now that I look at it - it all breaks the
> "own the loop" principle.

Agreed. The choice of which skills a session has belongs to us, made
before the model starts, and a deny list in claude's settings puts it
in the wrong place.

> I think the design then is a package that installs plugins to a known
> location. Then it has an interface for executing prompts, and selecting
> plugins to load. Something like:
>
>     skl p -p commit-first < prompt-file
>
> Start an RFC for a utility calld skl that lives in src/skl.

It is posted as "[RFC] skl: Run a prompt with a chosen set of skills"
[1]. It proposes:

- installing src/claude-plugins/* to share/skl/plugins/,
- "skl p [-p plugin[:skill]]... < prompt", which runs claude -p with
  only the selected skills loaded, and "skl ls" to list what is
  installed,
- passing claude's output and exit status through unread,
- a three-commit plan and four open questions.

> I think I'm seeing a distinction here... skills in .claude are skills
> that apply to that repo specifically. I may have some of that for the
> lab repo - but a lot of what I've been trying to do is develop skills
> that I can reuse in other contexts. If that's the case, they are not
> .claude/skills - they are src/claude-plugins/<name>.

The RFC uses that split as written: .claude/skills for skills about
this repo, and src/claude-plugins/<name> for reusable ones.

> At first I was thinking that I would have a different plugin per
> skill. Perhaps I can package several skills in a single plugin, and then
> my wrapper script produces the correct args to claude to override
> specific skills?

Several skills in one plugin works. Overriding them with claude
arguments does not. I tested it with Claude Code 2.1.296, using a
build plugin that holds by-commit and by-spec:

- Passing --settings with skillOverrides set to "off" left
  build:by-spec listed. I tried both "build:by-spec" and "by-spec" as
  the key, so overrides do not reach plugin skills.
- A temporary plugin dir with the same plugin.json and a symlink to
  by-commit alone listed only build:by-commit.

So the wrapper should build the selection, not override it. For each
run, skl makes a temporary plugin dir with only the chosen skills
linked in, and passes that with --plugin-dir. A skill you did not
select is in no directory claude reads. This is also an allow list,
which fixes the problem the deny list had.

Objective: a way to try workflows like build-by-commit and
build-by-spec side by side, without committing them to CLAUDE.md
